From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000118 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Codex <codex@local>
Date: Wed, 13 May 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] mips: sf2000: accept KSEG0 syscall stack

The 0077 hardware log still had all bFLT syscalls returning EFAULT, and the
new syscall-table dispatch markers were never written.  That means the error is
before syscall_common.  The raw init stack is KSEG0, but the SF2000 special
case only skipped extra stack argument loads for KSEG1 stacks.  The normal user
stack limit check then rejects the KSEG0 stack and branches to bad_stack.

Accept both KSEG0 and KSEG1 stack aliases for this temporary SF2000 NOMMU
bring-up path.  Also record the bad_stack stack pointer in the high raw diag
area so future logs can prove whether this path is still involved.
---
 arch/mips/kernel/scall32-o32.S | 12 ++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 10 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/arch/mips/kernel/scall32-o32.S b/arch/mips/kernel/scall32-o32.S
index 1111111..2222222 100644
--- a/arch/mips/kernel/scall32-o32.S
+++ b/arch/mips/kernel/scall32-o32.S
@@ -84,9 +84,9 @@ sf2000_after_syscall_irq_state:
 	lw	t0, PT_R29(sp)		# get old user stack pointer
 
 #ifdef CONFIG_MIPS_SF2000
-	li	t5, 0xe0000000
+	li	t5, 0xc0000000
 	and	t4, t0, t5
-	li	t5, 0xa0000000
+	li	t5, 0x80000000
 	beq	t4, t5, sf2000_kseg_stack_args
 	nop
 #endif
@@ -254,8 +254,15 @@ syscall_trace_entry:
 	 * failed. We probably should handle this case a bit more drastic.
 	 */
 bad_stack:
+#ifdef CONFIG_MIPS_SF2000
+	li	k0, 0xa1400000
+	li	k1, 0x51510537
+	sw	k1, 160(k0)
+	lw	k1, PT_R29(sp)
+	sw	k1, 164(k0)
+#endif
 	li	v0, EFAULT
 	sw	v0, PT_R2(sp)
 	li	t0, 1				# set error flag
 	sw	t0, PT_R7(sp)
 	j	o32_syscall_exit
-- 
2.45.0
